# ---- 5.6 Amazon: drop the duplicated title-instrument caveat (stated 3x) ----
REPL.append((
"The harm magnitude is lower on Amazon (0.22-0.50 vs 0.71-0.76) for two reasons we make explicit. First, public ESCI carries no `product_class`, so the category filter is matched to the product title rather than a clean category field; a title-based filter is a noisier, more permissive instrument that excludes fewer relevant items than a true category facet would. Second, the Amazon catalog is ~28x larger and far more heterogeneous, so a guessed category retains a larger absolute pool and a different recall denominator. The Amazon instrument is title-based and noisier than a clean category field, so its 0.22-0.50 recall loss is a different, weaker measurement; whether it under- or over-states the harm a real clean-category Amazon filter would inflict is not established. We therefore keep Amazon as a routing-replication domain and out of the headline harm range. On organic ESCI queries (where only brand and color facets are public), the same conservative-to-expansion rise in spurious injection appears (e.g. Llama 0.31 -> 0.82 of queries with a spurious constraint; Mistral 0.01 -> 0.55), so the over-injection is not specific to the category slot or to home goods.",
"The harm magnitude is lower on Amazon (0.22-0.50 vs 0.71-0.76) for two reasons: public ESCI has no `product_class`, so the filter is matched to the product title (a noisier, more permissive instrument that excludes fewer relevant items than a curated facet), and the catalog is ~28x larger and more heterogeneous, so a guessed category retains a larger pool. Whether the title-based 0.22-0.50 under- or over-states a real clean-category Amazon filter is not established, so we keep Amazon as a routing-replication domain, out of the headline range. On organic ESCI queries the same conservative-to-expansion rise in spurious injection appears (e.g. Llama 0.31->0.82 of queries; Mistral 0.01->0.55), so the over-injection is not specific to the category slot or to home goods."))

# ---- 6 downstream-ranker + multi-arm: merge two paragraphs into one ----
REPL.append((
"**A downstream ranker does not change this.** The harm sits in the hard *pre-filter*: a learned ranker only reorders the candidates it is handed, so it cannot recover items the filter already excluded, as we confirm empirically in Section 5.5 (a cross-encoder after the hard filter leaves a 0.20-0.43 recall@100 loss). The only ranker configuration that helps treats the category as a soft feature, which is the soft endpoint of the enforcement curve (Fig. 4).\n\n**Multi-arm retrieval bounds the harm.** Many modern systems use federated candidate generation, in which the emitted category seeds only one retrieval arm whose results are unioned with lexical and embedding arms before ranking. There the category filter never gates the whole candidate set, so the harm is bounded by the other arms, structurally the soft endpoint of the enforcement curve. The result is thus a warning specific to single-funnel hard-filter designs, and to the category arm's own contribution within a union, not to every production architecture.",
"**A downstream ranker and federated retrieval bound the harm; a single funnel does not.** The harm sits in the hard pre-filter, upstream of ranking: a learned ranker only reorders what it is handed and cannot recover excluded items (a cross-encoder after the filter leaves a 0.20-0.43 loss, Section 5.5). Federated systems, where the emitted category seeds one retrieval arm unioned with lexical and embedding arms before ranking, are likewise bounded by the other arms (the soft endpoint of the enforcement curve). The result is therefore a warning specific to single-funnel hard-filter designs, not to every production architecture; the only ranker configuration that helps treats the category as a soft feature."))

# ---- 6 generalization: tighten SQL detail (full detail already cited to scripts) ----
REPL.append((
"**Generalization: the failure is specific to structured slot-filling.** The mechanism predicts that a pipeline forcing an LLM to commit a single high-coverage structured value for an under-specified input, then applying it as a hard filter, is exposed, while free-form constraint generation is not. We tested one alternative modality on four local models. Prompting each planner to emit a SQL `WHERE` clause over a `products` table for the 55 type-less motifs, under the same aggressive narrowing instruction, the spurious category-equality rate (`category = 'guessed'`) is 0-22% (Qwen 2%, Mistral and Gemma 0%, Llama 22%), against ~100% category routing in the slot-filling schema. Three of the four write permissive multi-field `LIKE` disjunctions on the literal motif term (e.g. `title LIKE '%dinosaur%' OR ... OR category LIKE '%dinosaur%'`) rather than a narrowing equality; Llama is the most prone to a hard category equality but still far below its slot-filling rate. Given the freedom to write a disjunction, the models largely stay faithful. The mis-routing is therefore weaker in free-form generation, for these four models under this prompt; we do not claim it is absent from structured generation in general. The comparison is also not clean: the SQL planner can write a permissive `LIKE` disjunction, an operator the fixed slot schema does not offer, so part of the difference is the available operator, not only the slot-filling pressure. The result bounds the generalization rather than settling it: read positively, the protective factor is operator expressiveness, since a fixed single-value slot forces a commitment a free-form clause can avoid, the format-restriction effect of [Tam et al., 2024]. We therefore do not claim modality rigidity transfers from the SQL test alone (Fig. 5, `micro_sql.py` and `micro_sql_multi.py`). The rigid-schema metadata-filter case, however, we do measure directly in a deployed framework rather than leaving it a conjecture.",
"**Generalization: the protective factor is operator expressiveness.** The mechanism predicts that forcing an LLM to commit a single high-coverage structured value, then hard-filtering on it, is exposed, while free-form generation is not. Prompting the four local models to emit a SQL `WHERE` clause over a `products` table for the 55 motifs (same aggressive instruction), the spurious category-equality rate is 0-22% (vs ~100% category routing in the slot-filling schema): three of four write permissive `LIKE` disjunctions on the motif term rather than a narrowing equality (`micro_sql.py`, `micro_sql_multi.py`). The comparison is not clean, since SQL offers a disjunction operator the fixed slot schema does not, so the protective factor is operator expressiveness, not the modality, the format-restriction effect of [Tam et al., 2024]. The rigid-schema metadata-filter case, however, we measure directly in a deployed framework rather than leaving it a conjecture."))

# ---- 6 Limitations: 3 paragraphs -> 1 (n=55 / grade-as-intent / title-instrument each stated once) ----
REPL.append((
"**Limitations.** The primary limitation: the clean, curated-category harm magnitude rests on a single home-goods domain at n=55 motifs (only 55 mined motifs survive the relevance threshold), and it is conditioned on treating motif-relevance as shopper intent, which public data do not let us validate against clicks or conversions. The recall losses should be read under that grade-as-intent assumption. The routing behavior and the existence of substantial harm are externally validated on three further catalogs (the 1.2M-product Amazon catalog, and the consumer-electronics and apparel domains of Section 5.8, where the pooled expansion loss is 0.48 and 0.38), but those use a weaker title-based filter, so the clean curated-category magnitude itself is shown only on WANDS. The evaluation spans four domains (Wayfair home goods with graded relevance; the full 1.2M-product Amazon catalog; and consumer electronics and apparel, each with a title-based instrument).\n\nThe query-attribute reference is automatic (catalog-vocabulary tagging with normalization); we release a verification packet for optional manual auditing, but the reported numbers use the automatic reference, and the headline entity-probe results do not depend on it at all (their labels are known by construction). Harm uses deterministic conflict labels plus BM25 and dense retrieval; we additionally verify with a cross-encoder reranker that the loss survives a strong reranking stage (Section 5.5), but we do not evaluate a full proprietary production ranking stack, and recall@100 is a retrieval-quality proxy, not a direct business metric (conversion, revenue), though on entity queries the filter also lowers precision@10 and nDCG@10 (Section 5.5), so it does not trade recall for head-of-list precision there. Per-cell entity-probe n is 55 (WANDS) and 1,162 (Amazon); routing effects are at saturation (forced 0.98-1.00) and the recall-loss per-model 95% CIs are roughly +/-0.15. The entity-probe relevant set is non-matching by construction, so the enforcement calibration in Appendix A is calibration, not a behavioral result. The causal and mitigation results (the schema-vs-prompt dissociation, detector recovery, multi-class split, and the fix's precision cost) are demonstrated on the four local 4-bit models; the dissociation also reproduces at full bf16 precision (Section 5.5), so it is not a quantization artifact, but recovery, the multi-class split, and the precision cost are shown only at 4-bit. The five API models (plus two Gemini on WANDS) support the universality and recall-loss claims, and whether frontier models over-omit under the optional schema as Mistral does is untested. All models were evaluated through a uniform path (local 4-bit or provider API) at temperature 0; we did not evaluate web-product interfaces, which may wrap the base model in an additional query-understanding layer and behave differently. Reasoning depth was left at each provider's default rather than equalized across models, so the reasoning-capable models ran with their default internal thinking and the others without; we did not tune thinking budgets, and provider-internal thinking tokens are not fully captured in our token accounting. Broader external-validity checks (more e-commerce domains, more providers, and evaluation against a full production ranking stack rather than a cross-encoder reranker) remain useful next steps.\n\nThe WANDS home-goods catalog is favorable to the thesis, because motif queries cross product categories most there; we predicted and then measured (Section 5.8) that the harm magnitude is smaller in electronics (0.48) and apparel (0.38) where motifs cross fewer categories, which confirms the coverage mechanism but also means the clean WANDS magnitude is the high end of the range, not a typical one. Grocery and non-English queries are untested. The harm also assumes WANDS holistic Exact grades capture shopper intent for a motif; we do not validate this against clicks or conversions, and an alternative reading is that the grades are over-broad, so a category filter is a defensible refinement, a reading the 0.30 taxonomy-aware result partly speaks to. On statistics: per-cell n is 55 (WANDS) and 1,162 (Amazon), stated plainly; routing effects are at saturation (forced 0.98-1.00), so we rely on effect sizes and bootstrap intervals rather than p-values, and apply no multiple-comparison correction because no null-hypothesis test carries a headline claim.",
"**Limitations.** The clean, curated-category harm magnitude rests on a single home-goods domain at n=55 motifs, and is conditioned on treating motif-relevance as shopper intent, which public data do not let us validate against clicks or conversions; the losses should be read under that grade-as-intent assumption, and an alternative reading is that the holistic grades are over-broad and a category filter is a defensible refinement, a reading the 0.30 taxonomy-aware result partly speaks to. The routing and the existence of substantial harm are externally validated on three further catalogs (the 1.2M-product Amazon catalog, and electronics 0.48 / apparel 0.38 in Section 5.8), but those use a weaker title-based instrument, so only WANDS gives the clean magnitude, which is therefore the high end of the range, not a typical value. The query-attribute reference is automatic, but the headline entity-probe labels do not depend on it (they are known by construction). recall@100 is a retrieval proxy, not a business metric; on entity queries the filter also lowers precision@10 and nDCG@10 (against the same motif-defined grades). The causal and mitigation results (dissociation, detector recovery, multi-class split, precision cost) are on the four local 4-bit models; the dissociation reproduces at full bf16 precision, but recovery, the split, and the precision cost are shown only at 4-bit, and whether frontier models over-omit under the optional schema as Mistral does is untested. All models ran through a uniform local-or-API path at temperature 0 (no web interfaces), with reasoning depth left at each provider's default. Grocery and non-English queries are untested. Routing effects are at saturation (forced 0.98-1.00), so we rely on effect sizes and bootstrap intervals rather than p-values and apply no multiple-comparison correction, since no null test carries a headline claim."))

for i,(old,new) in enumerate(REPL):
    assert old in t, f"REPL {i} OLD not found: {old[:70]!r}"
